// Runa the Herbalist — the first conversation in the game, and the shape every
// later one is a bigger version of.
//
// Code holds the mechanics of walking a graph; this holds what is said and what
// saying it does. See `crate::systems::dialogue` for the rules, and
// `crate::assets::DialogueGraph` for what each field means.
//
// Three things about this file are load-bearing rather than decorative:
//
//   * `start` and every `next` are checked when the file is read, not when a
//     player reaches them. A typo here stops the map loading with a message
//     naming this graph and the node, rather than producing a conversation that
//     quietly ends early.
//
//   * A choice with a `condition` that does not hold is **hidden**, not greyed
//     out. "I brought your draught back." does not exist until you are actually
//     carrying one, which is the whole reason a fetch quest is a quest.
//
//   * `next: None` is how a reply ends the conversation. It is not the absence
//     of a target; it is the target.
//
// The errand is deliberately a loop rather than a line — `who` leads back to
// `greet`, and `greet` re-reads its conditions every time it is entered — so
// that "come back when you have it" is the same node offering a different set
// of replies, rather than a second copy of the greeting.
//
// Two errands run through that loop, and they are shaped differently on purpose:
//
//   quest.draught  M5's. Gated on the item alone, and repeatable — Runa never
//                  runs out of draughts, and asking twice works.
//   quest.helm     M6's, and the fetch quest proper. It has a *stage*, it is
//                  offered in one place and returned in another, and the reply
//                  that returns it needs the stage and the item at once. That
//                  conjunction is `All([...])`, and it is the only thing Q-2
//                  needed the condition enum to grow.
//
// Runa does not run out of draughts: the `gift` branch has no condition, so
// asking twice works. That is generosity in the fiction and an observable in
// the harness — the number of replies on offer goes from three to four the
// moment the player is carrying one, and `dialogue_choices` is what a tape
// watches to see a gated branch unlock.
Dialogue(
    id: "elder_intro",
    start: "greet",
    nodes: {
        "greet": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            // More than one line, so the paging is exercised by shipped
            // content rather than only by a test: `confirm` reads on, and the
            // replies are offered once the last line has been.
            lines: [
                "You are the one who came down out of the castle.",
                "Sit if you like. You have the look of someone who has been fighting.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(text: "Who are you?", next: "who"),
                Choice(text: "Have you anything for the road?", next: "gift"),
                Choice(
                    text: "I brought your draught back.",
                    next: "thanks",
                    condition: HasItem("minor_potion"),
                    effects: [
                        TakeItem("minor_potion", 1),
                        GiveItem("elder_charm", 1),
                        SetFlag("quest.draught.stage", 2),
                    ],
                ),
                // The errand's return leg. Two conditions rather than one, and
                // that is the whole of what Q-2 needed the engine to grow:
                // "she asked you for it" *and* "you are carrying one". Gated on
                // `HasItem` alone it would be offered to someone who has never
                // spoken to her about it, which is a reply that answers a
                // question nobody asked; gated on the flag alone it would be
                // offered empty-handed. `FlagEq(1)` rather than `FlagAtLeast(1)`
                // because stage 1 is "asked for and not yet delivered": handing
                // the helm over sets the stage to 2, and that alone is what
                // stops Runa buying a second helm off you forever.
                Choice(
                    text: "I have the helm you asked for.",
                    next: "helm",
                    condition: All([
                        FlagEq("quest.helm.stage", 1),
                        HasItem("knight_helm"),
                    ]),
                    effects: [
                        TakeItem("knight_helm", 1),
                        GiveItem("iron_band", 1),
                        SetFlag("quest.helm.stage", 2),
                    ],
                ),
                Choice(text: "Not now.", next: None),
            ],
        ),
        "who": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            lines: [
                "Runa. I keep what is left of this village on its feet.",
                "There were forty of us before the knights came down.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(text: "I am sorry.", next: "greet"),
                // Where the errand is offered, and deliberately not at `greet`.
                // Runa mentions the knights here and nowhere else, so the quest
                // comes out of the one line that sets it up rather than being a
                // fourth reply to a greeting; and — the practical half — `greet`
                // is the node `tapes/dialogue_branch.tape` counts the replies
                // of, so an unconditional choice added there would move a number
                // that tape and its golden trace already pin.
                //
                // `FlagEq(0)` rather than `FlagAtLeast`: once you have taken the
                // errand there is nothing left to ask, and once you have done it
                // there is nothing left to take.
                Choice(
                    text: "Which of them came down here?",
                    next: "errand",
                    condition: FlagEq("quest.helm.stage", 0),
                ),
            ],
        ),
        "gift": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            lines: [
                "Take this, then. Bring back what you do not drink — glass is dear here.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(
                    text: "I will.",
                    next: None,
                    effects: [
                        GiveItem("minor_potion", 1),
                        SetFlag("quest.draught.stage", 1),
                    ],
                ),
            ],
        ),
        "thanks": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            lines: [
                "Kept whole. Not many would have bothered.",
                "Here — my mother's charm. It keeps the cold out.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(text: "Thank you.", next: None),
            ],
        ),
        // The errand itself: taking it is the only thing in this file that sets
        // `quest.helm.stage` to 1, and `greet` is where handing the helm over
        // sets it to 2. Two stages and one flag, which is the whole reason a
        // flag is an integer — `started` and `done` as two booleans would need a
        // third condition somewhere to rule out both being true at once.
        "errand": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            lines: [
                "One of them wore my husband's helm out of this village.",
                "I do not want the man. I want the helm off him.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(
                    text: "I will bring it to you.",
                    next: "greet",
                    effects: [SetFlag("quest.helm.stage", 1)],
                ),
                Choice(text: "That is not my errand.", next: "greet"),
            ],
        ),
        "helm": Node(
            speaker: "Runa the Herbalist",
            lines: [
                "That is his. I would know the dent anywhere.",
                "Take the band off my hand for it. It was his too.",
            ],
            choices: [
                Choice(text: "I am sorry for your loss.", next: None),
            ],
        ),
    },
)
